Singapore’s tourism sector is experiencing a strong recovery with inbound tourists up 19 times compared to 2021.
(Singapore Tourism Board announces that international visitors to Singapore in 2023 will number between 12 million and 14 million, generating approximately S$18 billion to S$21 billion in tourism revenue; visitor numbers and tourism revenues will return to around 67-75% of pre-COVID levels in 2019; there is great likelihood for full recovery of the tourism sector in 2024.)
The Singapore tourism market gradually reopens and recovers in 2022.
The latest statistics released by the Singapore Tourism Board show that incoming tourists will increase 19 times from 330,600 in 2021 to 6.3 million in 2022, exceeding the Board’s earlier forecast of 4 to 6 million, and that tourists have recovered to 33 percent of their pre-epidemic levels.
Singapore’s tourism revenue totaled 8.96 billion Singapore dollars from January to September 2022, and is projected to reach 13.8-14.3 billion Singapore dollars for the full year of 2022, up six- to seven-fold from 2021’s 1.89 billion Singapore dollars, and has recovered to about 50-52% of its pre-COVID-19 level of 2019.
The Government of Singapore has taken a number of measures to accelerate the recovery of the tourism sector.
In April 2022, the government announced that it would allocate around $490 million to stimulate tourism recovery.
The Tourism Board recently announced plans to allocate 110 million dollars from this budget over the next two years to launch more business and leisure activities to attract returning tourists.
To make healthy living one of Singapore &apos; s tourist highlights, the Singapore Tourism Board launched a call for interest on 23 November 2022 to develop a healthy living destination on Singapore &apos; s south coast.
Wang Ling Li, Executive Director of Sports and Wellness Tourism at the Singapore Tourism Board, said that in recent years, more and more tourists have put physical health at the top of their list of considerations.
Developing a health and wellness attraction in the South Beach area, with its Marina Bay Sands and Marina Barrages, has the potential to contribute to Singapore’s tourism and economy, encouraging visitors to spend more time there.
The Director of the Singapore Tourism Authority, Chen Jianlong, said that health and wellness would become an important part of Singapore &apos; s tourism product in the coming years.
The pandemic has raised public awareness and demand for physical and mental health.
For this reason, the Tourism Board has been promoting health and wellness programs to the local public and international tourists for the past two years.
In June 2022, the Singapore Tourism Board held the first Health and Wellness Festival, providing more than 130 different types of health activities and experiences for the public.
Singapore will continue to offer new travel experiences to its visitors.
In addition to the newly opened Singapore Children &apos; s Museum, the Avatar World at the Marina Bay Gardens, the Central Beach Marketplace at the Santa Monica Beach, the new &quot; Explore New Frontiers &quot; exhibition hall at the Museum of Art and Science and the new open-air theatre at the Night Wildlife Park, the Tourism Board will support the development and renewal of projects such as the Bird Park at the World of Birds, which will make Singapore an attractive international travel destination.
According to the plan, Singapore will develop industry standards for sustainable development of meetings, exhibitions and incentive tourism in 2023, with particular focus on waste management and carbon emissions reduction issues, further optimizing the travel environment.
The recovery of Singapore's tourism industry has accelerated the recovery of the aviation industry.
Data shows that as of December 18, 2022, Changi Airport has seen a strong recovery in passenger traffic, with daily passengers reaching over 150,000 and weekly numbers around 1 million, equivalent to about 75% of pre-pandemic levels, and the number of employees back at work is also up to approximately 90% of their pre-pandemic level.
Data show that Singapore has received 23.6 million passengers between January and October 2022, 42% more than before the pandemic, and 170,000 flights, 53% more than before the pandemic.
Tourism’s rapid rebound has also spurred recovery in sectors such as accommodation, transportation, dining, and shopping.
The Singapore government and the tourism industry are optimistic about the Chinese market.
According to Ko Suk Tan, Assistant Director of the International Division of the Singapore Tourism Board, a survey by China Travel Platform showed that Singapore ranks among the top three in the world for safety, which is one of the reasons why Singapore is so popular with Chinese tourists.
Before the outbreak of the new coronavirus, about 3.6 million Chinese tourists visited Singapore every year.
With China relaxing its border policy, Chinese tourists are expected to return this year to 30-60% of their 2019 levels.
Ko Su-kyung said that the Tourism Board will now closely monitor further signals released by China on outbound travel policies to respond to changing tourist demands.
In addition to strengthening partnerships with Chinese online travel platforms and travel agencies such as China Travel, the Singapore Tourism Board will actively prepare to welcome Chinese tourists in response to the emerging trends in traveling abroad.
Looking ahead to the 2023 tourism market, the Singapore Tourism Board is confident that the recovery will be faster than expected, provided that there is no significant recession in the global economy that affects public revenues, and no new large-scale, deadly outbreaks.
A quick guide to Internet Finance
The book is shallow, and it can be said to be just an introduction, which can help you quickly understand the whole picture of Internet finance, but to understand it in depth, you still have to follow the diagram, and dig deep.
And it was written earlier, and the content of the later WeChat and Alipay subsidy war to cultivate user habits has not been mentioned. The proportion of third-party payment is probably not as small as it is mentioned in the book now.
Nowadays, many people take their phones out to eat instead of carrying cash, and banks have also abandoned UnionPay due to high bank transaction volumes.
But still, I am very grateful to the author for writing this introductory book; it is evident that the author has had many years of financial observation experience and is familiar with both domestic and international financial development histories, which allows him/her to write in such an easy-to-understand manner.
Writing highbrow is easy; making it accessible is hard.
