And in the part about the competition, I wrote down some feelings of nostalgia for the passage of time and the old-new transition (perhaps because I am melancholic).
It took me much of my senior year to read the whole book, but I couldn't stop reading it.
The full-time character and the emotional line are very impressive, and some characters who only appear occasionally will leave a deep impression on the readers, such as the protagonist Ye Xiaocheng and the supporting character Huang Shitian, and the characters who are not in the center of the writing, such as Jiang Bo and Chu Yunxi, are also very popular. In a novel about esports, there is no character that the readers hate to the extreme, which is the success of the full-time character in the character portrayal.
And on the emotional front, full-time has no of course problems with the afterglow girls in general web novels that have been criticized for being either beautiful or perverted; all the characters’ emotions share one common feature – they like Glory and most want to win honorably.
Small measures have no great conspiracy; everyone looks so simple and adorable.
This is perhaps why there are no unlikable full-time characters.
This is also due to the large number of full-time IPs.
Setting aside the phenomenon of web novels, this is a good book.
How much of him really gives some blood and a little bit of emotion to people?
I will be full-time in animation this April.
