I admire you, and I want you to know it!
The first factor that determines an employee’s job satisfaction is not salary but whether they feel sufficiently valued and appreciated in their work.
According to Maslow’s hierarchy of needs, after the basic survival needs are met, the most urgent needs are psychological needs – the desire to be understood, acknowledged, and appreciated.
Indeed, for the Chinese people, most of them “love you in my heart but can’t say it out loud.” Even if they have love, gratitude, and admiration, they keep these feelings inside without saying anything.
In this book, the author discusses five languages of appreciation: affirmative words, thoughtful moments, acts of service, gifts of appreciation, and physical contact.
I think it is not important that you know what the five languages of appreciation are, but that you can use them from within, anytime, anywhere, to express appreciation and gratitude to those around you, whether they are family, friends or colleagues.
In fact, the closer we are to someone, the more we need to appreciate and be grateful, so that our lives become better.
Appreciation can help companies retain talent to some extent and reduce turnover rates.
Thanks to the translator for bringing us wonderful foreign ideas and worlds.
Due to cultural backgrounds in China and Western countries, there is likely to be some difference between languages, so if you have an opportunity to see the original version of the author, I believe you will gain more.
Once again, thank you to the authors and translators, as well as to the editors, for their hard work!
A customer's confession
I received this defective product, and Amazon customer service told me not to worry, we will contact you within 54 hours.
2 Then in the middle you contact the customer service staff, who will tell you that the matter will be handled by a commissioner, who will inform you of the outcome.
You never contact the Commissioner, you never know her office number, you never have a direct line, so you can't contact her, only she can contact you.
Tragedy is that I waited for nearly three days, and this official called me; however, my phone was away from me for a while, so I did not receive his call.
I waited for two hours without getting that specialist's call; I then contacted Amazon customer service and sent an email to them asking her to call me back.
It has been four days since I received this defective coffee maker from Amazon and still don’t know what kind of treatment it will receive!
Amazon goods in the United States are fine, and overseas shopping is a good way to shop, but it is far from the service for Asia.
Six is not enough for me; I can only continue to wait...
Not very recommended reading
I've read it a long time ago, and I just saw it in memory.
Rather than a collection of short stories, the book is a collection of fragments of life about love.
The tone of this book is extremely plain; it’s like drinking water – bland and unassuming. The language used is minimalist to an extreme degree, with every word chosen for its impact on the reader. The plot is as quiet and unassuming as a summer afternoon in the city streets.
Each short story is a fragment of a moment in ordinary life, without a beginning or an end.
Finally, they were collected together under the title What We Are Talking About When We Talk About Love.
I think that this collection of novels, and its title, is like an act of art, in which the author wants to express that love is more than a tragic union on stage, a dry sea in urban legends, or a cold-blooded one-on-one, that love is more than that and should not be represented by that, but that it is also a life of watery plains, a dull day after day, but also a life of helplessness, an escape from reality, and uncertainty about the future.
As you read this book, memories of arguing couples beside you, uninteresting marriages with acquaintances, and unsatisfactory relationships may come to mind.
That is why I do not recommend reading it. It is aesthetically dull, both in writing and in plot, and unless your life is so rippled or sweet as living in a candy castle, you must feel strange and boring when you read it.
And in terms of depth of thought, everyday situations do not surprise or expand one’s mind and perspective (this is unlike in Gabriel Garcia Marquez’s Love in the Time of Cholera), while brief episodes fail to address any issues, such as what kind of mistake led to the failure of love?
What caused the breakup of the main character's marriage?
So I don't recommend reading this book, it doesn't matter if you read it or not.
The author's ideas are expressed more through techniques and genres than through narrative, which is also a reason for the poor reading experience.
